      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to evaluate the use of vaginal misoprostol to decrease both the force required to dilate the cervix and the pain experienced during a hysteroscopy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Randomized clinical trial (RCT) (Canadian Task Force classification I)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "University hospital gynecology clinic."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "A total of 101 patients needing a diagnostic hysteroscopy. Fifty patients were randomized to the misoprostol group and 51 to the placebo group. Patient characteristics were similar in the 2 groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Self-administration of 400 microg of vaginal misoprostol or vaginal placebo 12 to 24 hours before a hysteroscopy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "The force needed to dilate the cervix was assessed by a tonometer, and pain was measured by a visual analog scale. The force to dilate the cervix to 6mm was significantly less in the misoprostol group (5.0 vs 7.5N, p=.02). Pain-related measurements after dilatation of the cervix to 6mm were significantly reduced in the misoprostol group (42.1 vs 57.2, p=.004). The main side effect reported with the use of the drug was pelvic cramping."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The use of 400 microg of vaginal misoprostol 12 to 24 hours before hysteroscopy reduces the pain and the force needed to dilate the cervix, with only mild side effects."
        }
      ]},
